::SPEECH_U05_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U05_L02_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Clarity and cohesion item 1: Which choice best revises speech language for audience understanding?
{
~A. Use vague words that do not name the idea#This choice is incomplete because it does not improve the speech draft in a clear, audience-focused way.
~B. Put sentences together with no connection#This choice may sound related, but it does not fully address revision, clarity, cohesion, or standard speech communication.
=C. Use precise wording that names the idea clearly#Correct. This choice improves the speech by using feedback, clarity, cohesion, or standard English to support audience understanding.
~D. Add transitions without explaining the relationship#This choice weakens communication because it ignores how the audience will understand the speech.
}
::SPEECH_U05_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U05_L02_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Clarity and cohesion item 2: Which choice best revises speech language for audience understanding?
{
=A. Connect ideas with transitions that show relationships#Correct. This choice improves the speech by using feedback, clarity, cohesion, or standard English to support audience understanding.
~B. Put sentences together with no connection#This choice may sound related, but it does not fully address revision, clarity, cohesion, or standard speech communication.
~C. Use informal wording in a formal presentation#This choice reflects a common misconception. Recheck the feedback or sentence connection before revising.
~D. Add transitions without explaining the relationship#This choice weakens communication because it ignores how the audience will understand the speech.
}
